<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabban Sauma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–1294</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Rabban Bar Sauma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabban Sauma was a Turkic monk of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>), who travelled west on pilgrimage and became an envoy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a>. In 1287–1288 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> and Pope Nicholas IV, to seek an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. His account of the journey survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabban-sauma.jpg" alt="Rabban Sauma" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VoyagesOfRabbanBarSauma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
